.SS Execution model
.PP
Without \fB\-x\fR, \fBwatch\fR concatenates its trailing arguments
into a single string and passes them to \fBsh \-c\fR. This is what
makes shell features (pipes, redirection, globs) work, but it also
means quoting matters — arguments containing spaces are reflowed.
With \fB\-x\fR the command is \fBexecvp\fRed directly, with each
trailing argument passed verbatim and no shell involvement.
.SS Exit status
.PP
\fBwatch\fR exits with status \fB0\fR after \fBq\fR, Ctrl+C,
\fB\-\-chgexit\fR or \fB\-\-equexit\fR. After a command error,
\fB\-\-errexit\fR freezes the display until any single key is pressed,
then exits with status \fB1\fR. A newline is not required.
.PP
Keyboard exit and \fBSIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM\fR, or \fBSIGHUP\fR
interrupt a running command and reap it after terminating its process
group. Terminal input modes and line wrapping are restored before
returning. These three signals exit with status 0, matching procps-ng.
.SS Scheduling
.PP
By default, the interval starts after a command finishes.
\fB\-\-precise\fR includes the command's running time in the interval.
.SS Current behavioural caveats
.PP
\fB\-\-color\fR and \fB\-\-no\-color\fR currently affect line
truncation behaviour under \fB\-\-no\-wrap\fR but do not interpret or
strip ANSI escape sequences themselves; whatever escape codes the
command emits will reach the terminal as-is. If your command's output
should be presented colour-stripped, pipe it through \fBsed\fR or
\fBansifilter\fR(1) first.
